Since 20 people like both apples and oranges, 60 - 20 = 40 people like only apples, and 40 - 20 = 20 people like only oranges. Therefore, the answer is 10.

We can use a Venn diagram to help visualize the problem. Let B represent the set of students who like basketball, and let S represent the set of students who like soccer. We know that n(B) = 60, n(S) = 40, and n(B S) = 20. The number of students who like only basketball is given by n(B) - n(B S) = 60 - 20 = 40. 

